nd throughout Eora's Circles, while cities have domesticated and working animals. &nbsp;Wheat, potatoes, and meat are the basis of many meals in Eora. Rice is less common, and is associated with wealth and status. Manufactured ingredients are considered the norm, while less processed foods are seen as luxury. Pure water is also considered a luxury, so diluted water (or thinwater) is the kingdom's main source of drinking and household water. &nbsp;In Eora, death is viewed as a journey rather than an ending. Common depictions of reaching the afterlife include the dead drifting along a large body of water, and being drawn towards the moon. There are no confirmed accounts of what exists past being guided to the moon. Traditional burials are very rare, and are reserved only for the Queens. Cremation is the most common.&nbsp;\nOther Names: The Pouring, Blessing of the Waterbearer\nThis occurs once every eight years. On this day, the Well is opened to the public and is replenished by the <a data-tooltip-position=\"top\" aria-label=\"Tempest, the Waterbearer\" data-href=\"Tempest, the Waterbearer\" href=\"2.-world/2.3-religion/2.31-true-gods/tempest,-the-waterbearer.html\" class=\"internal-link\" target=\"_self\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Waterbearer</a>.Occurs once every academic year. Is a two-week long holiday where magical and non-magical schools suspend class. ","aliases":[],"inlineTags":[],"frontmatterTags":[],"headers":[{"heading":"Daily Life","level":1,"id":"Daily_Life_0"},{"heading":"Food &amp; Drink","level":1,"id":"Food_&_Drink_0"},{"heading":"Death","level":1,"id":"Death_0"},{"heading":"Holidays","level":1,"id":"Holidays_0"},{"heading":"The Waterbearer's Blessing","level":5,"id":"The_Waterbearer's_Blessing_0"},{"heading":"Spring Recess","level":5,"id":"Spring_Recess_0"}],"links":["2.-world/2.3-religion/2.31-true-gods/tempest,-the-waterbearer.html"],"author":"","coverImageURL":"","fullURL":"2.-world/2.7-lore/2.71-society/society.html","pathToRoot":"../../..","att